==============================================================================
D.  Headline claims
==============================================================================
  D1  'adjacent deep integers jump with d^2 in {8,10,12}'
      observed on 1000033..1000050 : [8, 10, 12, 14]
      verdict: FALSE as stated (values [8, 10, 12, 14] occur)
      also: under the pure Gray (shift) encoder consecutive integers
      differ in exactly one bit, d^2_raw = 1 -- the {8,10,12} values
      come from the factor encoder, not from lattice geometry
      raw d^2 under the shift encoder: [1]

  D2  'even quantisation d^2 in 2Z at 100%'
      odd jump norms in 2999 consecutive transitions: 0
      every legacy-snapped state has even weight: True
      verdict: TRUE, and it is a theorem, not data.  Golay is doubly
      even, so Hamming-weight parity is constant on each coset; the
      cosets the legacy snap fails on are exactly those with weight-4
      leaders (even).  Hence every snapped state has even weight and
      every d^2 is even -- for ANY encoder and ANY integers, primes
      or not.  It carries no information about primality.

  D3  'd^2 = 8 steps are Class-B minimal-vector octad hops'
      TRUE for genuine codewords, but with the legacy snap
      141/300 states are not codewords, so the classification of
      the published table is accidental (norms 0,2,4,6 are impossible
      between real codewords: minimum distance is 8)
      With the complete decoder every d^2 = 8 step is an exact
      minimal (norm-32) Leech vector -- see part F.

==============================================================================
E.  Propeller imbalance as a prime detector
==============================================================================
  definition: coefficient of variation of log p over the DISTINCT
  prime factors of n (so it ignores exponents entirely).
  composites in [1000000,1010000) : 9247
    with imbalance < 0.1500 (claimed impossible) : 248 (2.68%)
    with imbalance < 0.0010 ('Smooth', as primes) : 1
    example 1005973 = 997 × 1009         imbalance 0.000866  (Smooth)
    example 1018081 = 1009^2             imbalance 0.000000  (Smooth)
    example 1048576 = 2^20               imbalance 0.000000  (Smooth)
  verdict: 'primes 0.0000' is TRUE but vacuous (every prime power
  gives 0 too); 'composites > 0.1500' is FALSE.  The statistic
  measures the spread of the distinct prime factors, not primality.

